[00:35.26]Conversation One
[00:37.71]W: Tell me frankly, what are you afraid of?
[00:40.89]M: I'm not sure.
[00:42.18]I used to be scared of a lot of things such as dogs and fire when I was young, but as I grow up, not anymore.
[00:50.26]W: Are you afraid of death?
[00:52.49]M: Only if I think about it so much.
[00:54.94]My friend told me not to think too much about it.
[00:58.27]Death is inevitable, so there is no use thinking about it, right?
[01:02.80]W: How about horror movies?
[01:04.98]M: No. I know horror films are designed to frighten and panic, cause dread and alarm, and to invoke our hidden worst fears, often in a terrifying, shocking plot.
[01:17.32]W: At the same time, they are aimed at attracting and entertaining us in a unique experience, and they effectively center on the dark side of life, the forbidden, and strange and alarming events.
[01:30.02]M: Yes.
[01:30.91]They deal with our most original nature and its fears such as our nightmares, our vulnerability, our alienation, our disgust, our terror of the unknown, our fear of death, so they are all fake to a degree and there is nothing to be afraid of.
[01:49.09]How about you?
[01:50.66]W: I get scared pretty easily.
[01:52.65]I'm actually frightened of bees.
[01:54.78]M: Really? Why?
[01:56.48]W: I don't know.
[01:58.22]I got stung when I was young, and ever since I've always been afraid of them.
[02:02.83]M: What other things are you afraid of?
[02:05.73]W: Sitting by the camp fire and telling horror stories is very scary.
[02:10.56]M: Wow. You're afraid of many things, huh?
[02:13.77]W: I guess so.
[02:15.32]Aren't you afraid of something coming out of the bushes?
[02:18.24]M: It's not going to happen.
[02:20.13]We probably went camping dozens of times and nothing ever came out of the bushes.
[02:25.53]What makes you think something will?
[02:27.66]W: I don't know.
[02:28.84]I guess I'm terrified at the possibility.
[02:31.80]M: If you were realistic, you probably wouldn't be scared of everything.
[02:36.02]Maybe you have too much imagination.
[02:38.76]W: I should try to think more realistically.
[02:41.42]I'll do that.
[02:42.79]M: Good. After a while, you won't be afraid of so many things.
[02:49.29]Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[02:54.94]1. What is this conversation mainly about?
[03:12.25]2. Why is the man NOT afraid of horror movies?
[03:30.51]3. What frightens the woman according to the conversation?
[03:48.36]4. Why is the woman afraid of so many things according to the man?
[04:08.38]Conversation Two
[04:10.71]W: Good afternoon, Mr. Smith.
[04:12.70]Welcome to our program¡ªProtection of Animals.
[04:15.94]I know you have been involved in this field for many years.
[04:19.21]What triggered your dedication to it in the first place?
[04:22.44]M: Well, I guess it's the inborn love for animals.
[04:26.37]When I was a kid, my family was surrounded by all kinds of pets¡ªbirds, cats, and dogs.
[04:33.87]W: I see.
[04:35.07]But it is quite strange that for a nation of pet-lovers, Britain has surprisingly few vegetarians¡ªjust 3% of the population, isn't it?
[04:44.14]M: Yes. To make things worse, our animal experimentation laboratories boast lower welfare standards than many of the other countries', which is rather sad to see.
[04:55.88]W: I guess that is why the number of peaceful protests against institutions that perform research on animals has increased markedly lately.
[05:03.97]M: Yes.
[05:04.87]Peaceful protection is one of our means to show our anger and objection against animal experimentation.
[05:11.60]We have also formed some powerful lobby groups to persuade people against brutal animal experimentation.
[05:18.81]W: Sounds quite encouraging.
[05:20.97]Do you get satisfactory results then?
[05:23.87]M: Sadly though, there is more for us to shout about.
[05:27.86]Despite a ban on the testing of cosmetics and household products on animals, the number of experiments performed in Britain rose by almost 40% between 2000 and 2010 to 3.7 million, while in France just 2.5 million experiments were conducted in 2010 and the figure has been stable for many years.
[05:53.86]W: It is sad to hear that.
[05:55.57]The reason for this increase may be that in some cases only animal tests are deemed sufficient.
[06:01.84]I heard that changes to the strict laws that require British scientists to consider alternatives to animal tests may be partially relaxed as a result of European reforms.
[06:13.02]M: Yes.
[06:13.82]We have made an open protest against that legislation too.
[06:18.03]The Home Office is due to respond to our protest on this subject within the next few weeks.
[06:23.79]W: I hope the officials would listen to you.
[06:26.24]Is there any other project in your program?
[06:28.86]M: We have set up Animal Aid, an organization targeting medical-research charities.
[06:35.01]We wish that it can bring us suitable solutions in the near future.
[06:41.23]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[06:46.73]5. What caused the man to devote to the protection of animals?
[07:05.20]6. Why is there a significant rise in the number of peaceful protests against animal experimentation?
[07:26.13]7. How does the man view their lobby groups' achievements?
[07:44.10]8. Besides peaceful protests, what else did the man's organization do to protect animals?
