[08:36.65]Passage One
[08:38.55]In the rush to reinvent the way we live, do the tech reformers forget the negative impact some of their miraculous products can have on the lives and jobs of many people?
[08:48.53]Take facial recognition as an example.
[08:51.26]Have you ever come across someone whose face you recognized but you couldn't remember his or her name?
[08:56.69]It's always happening to me.
[08:58.90]The augmented reality company Blippar already allows you to scan objects with your smartphone's camera to find out more about them¡ªnow you will be able to scan faces too.
[09:11.32]But facial recognition software can be controversial, and this could be another innovation which sounds great in a brainstorming session but has unintended consequences in the real world.
[09:23.02]We hear from Blippar's co-founder Omar Tayeb who tells us how the app works, and why we shouldn't be worried about its implications for privacy.
[09:32.05]Another story is about another area where the tech industry's reputation is coming into question¡ªthe fake news spread over social networks and via Google searches.
[09:42.21]The tech firms are now starting to help readers understand what is true and what is not, but what can the traditional media firms do?
[09:50.31]Critics of the mainstream media will argue that the problem started long before many people got their news online.
[09:56.93]So whose job is it now to decide what is true, and put clear markings on fake news?
[10:02.52]You might think that was the function of editors, but Alex Sander of The Guardian tells us "it's the reader's job to mark things as fake."
[10:12.58]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[10:18.89]9. What can we learn about the inventions from tech reformers?
[10:38.02]10. What does the speaker say about facial recognition software?
[10:55.90]11. Whose job is it to mark fake news according to Alex Sander?
[11:16.55]Passage Two
[11:19.34]Ask any American who invented the airplane and they would say the Wright brothers, but Brazilians would have a different answer.
[11:28.04]In the Opening Ceremony of the Olympics on Friday, Brazil continued that claim by honoring Alberto Santos-Dumont, the man they credit with the invention.
[11:39.96]In 1906, Santos-Dumont flew his plane on the outskirts of France and was recognized around Europe as the creator of the airplane.
[11:50.11]But Orville and Wilbur Wright later proved that their first flight came three years earlier in 1903.
[11:57.78]The question is whether the Wright brothers' flight should count.
[12:01.53]The opinion of many Brazilians is that the Wrights used a catapult and simply created a glider that could maintain a prolonged fall.
[12:10.77]In 2003, CNN looked into the conflicting claims.
[12:15.52]Aviation experts in Brazil claim that the Wright brothers needed strong and steady winds to get the "Kitty Hawk" off the ground, leaving no proof that it was able to take off on its own.
[12:28.57]American experts disagree with that claim and say the Wrights' plane had to have been self-sustaining.
[12:36.07]"Even in 1903 the airplane sustained itself in the air for nearly one minute. If it's not sustaining itself under its own power, it's not going to stay up that long," Peter Jakab of the National Air and Space Museum told CNN.
[12:53.08]Which country really invented the airplane depends plenty on the definition and on the circumstances that the Wright brothers used to get off the ground.
[13:03.22]The country of Brazil made it clear which side of the debate it is on early in the Opening Ceremony.
[13:11.23]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[13:16.08]12. What can we learn about Santos-Dumont?
[13:33.73]13. What hinders the confirmation of the creator of the airplane according to the speaker?
[13:53.99]14. Why do Brazilians think that the Wright brothers' flight doesn't count as the first one?
[14:13.95]15. What was the justification that American experts argued for the Wright brothers' flight?
