[19:00.31]Recording Two
[19:02.85]In 2012, the Mangalore City Corporation banned the manufacture, sale, and distribution of plastic bags.
[19:10.89]Other Indian cities and provinces have followed suit, and the country as a whole considered a ban back in February of this year.
[19:20.06]It sounds like a good thing (and it is, for the environment), but local entrepreneur Ashwath Hegde knew that not everyone would be able to afford the available alternatives.
[19:30.98]So he did what anyone would do¡ªhe spent four years knee-deep in research and emerged with EnviGreen¡ªa company that produces organic, biodegradable, eco-friendly bags.
[19:43.58]The bags are made from 12 ingredients, including potato, corn, vegetable oil, banana, and flower oil, so don't be fooled even though they look very similar to their plastic predecessors.
[19:57.24]If you place an EnviGreen bag in a glass of room-temperature water, it will completely dissolve in a day (in boiling water, it will dissolve in just 15 seconds).
[20:07.82]The bags will biodegrade in less than 180 days, so they can be tossed without worry, and¡ªthis is the best part¡ª they're edible£¬which means that if an animal happens to ingest them, they would be perfectly fine.
[20:21.81]Amazing, no? But wait.
[20:23.86]It gets better.
[20:25.15]Even the process for making these bags is completely chemical free.
[20:29.80]"We don't use any chemicals at all. Even the paint used for printing on the bags is natural and organic. And the cost of one EnviGreen bag is about 35% more than that of a plastic bag, but 500% less than a cloth bag."
[20:46.06]And he even drank the water that dissolved one of his bags to prove that it's safe.
[20:51.00]Hegde's claims are backed up by the Karnataka State Pollution Control Board, which conducted several tests to ensure the bags contained no plastic before approving their use.
[21:01.73]They don't melt, drop, or release any toxic fumes when burnt, and the results have been verified by both the Central Institute of Plastic Engineering and the Sriram Institute for Industrial Research.
[21:14.85]Hegde has set up a factory in Mangalore City and he plans to source his materials from local farmers.
[21:21.50]At the moment, the bags are not available in India, but are in Qatar and Abu Dhabi.
[21:27.44]They will be in India soon and, with cities like Los Angeles already banning plastic bags in the US, hopefully they'll make their way across the world soon.
[21:39.05]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[21:45.32]19. What can we learn about EnviGreen?
[22:03.37]20. What is the best part of EnviGreen bags?
[22:22.18]21. What does Ashwath Hegde say about EnviGreen bags?
[22:42.45]Recording Three
[22:45.36]Archaeologists have discovered at least 58 shipwrecks filled with ancient objects in the Aegean Sea between Greece and Turkey. 
[22:54.17]The discovery is believed to be the largest concentration of wrecks ever found in the Mediterranean. 
[23:00.59]The sunken ships were found near the islands of Fournoi. 
[23:04.43]They gathered there over a long time period, from the time of ancient Greece to the 20th century. 
[23:10.76]Most of the ships are from the time of the Greek, Roman and Byzantine civilizations.
[23:16.59]Peter Campbell is leading the underwater research of the wrecks for the RPM Nautical Foundation, a group that studies ancient objects. 
[23:27.43]Campbell said, ¡°The excitement is difficult to describe, I mean, it was just incredible. 
[23:34.89]We knew that we had stumbled upon something that was going to change the history books.¡± 
[23:40.12]The international team began the underwater study in 2015. 
[23:45.37]They found 22 shipwrecks that year. 
[23:48.60]That number has climbed to 58 with the latest discovery. 
[23:53.10]The team believes there are even more secrets lying on the seabed below. 
[23:58.26]Campbell called it ¡°one of the top archaeological discoveries of the century.¡± 
[24:03.33]He told Reuters, ¡°we now have a new story to tell of a navigational route that connected the ancient Mediterranean.¡± 
[24:12.97]The wrecks and their contents show ships carrying goods from nearby areas like the Black Sea, Greece, Turkey, Armenia and Italy. 
[24:23.18]Other goods come from places farther away such as Spain, Sicily, Cyprus, Egypt, the Middle East and North Africa. 
[24:33.27]The team has raised more than 300 ancient objects from the shipwrecks, including many tall jars with two handles used by ancient Greek and Roman people to carry liquid. 
[24:45.72]George Koutsouflakis is with the Greek government's Ephorate of Underwater Antiquities. 
[24:51.88]He said, ¡°the tall jar is a vessel used mainly for transporting liquids and semi-liquids in antiquity, so the goods it would be transporting were mostly wine, oil, fish sauces, perhaps honey.¡± 
[25:07.10]He added that fish sauce from the Black Sea region was an expensive good in ancient times.
[25:13.82]Then, how did they get there? 
[25:16.95]Well, bad weather is the most likely explanation for why the ships all sank in the same area near Fournoi, a stopover point for ships to spend the night. 
[25:28.12]The region also experiences a lot of sudden, fierce storms and is surrounded by rocky shores. 
[25:35.96]In later times, pirates were also active in the area, attacking ships filled with expensive goods. 
[25:43.86]Because of the discoveries, researchers want to create a center for underwater archaeology in Fournoi with a museum to show their finds.
